  "query_preview": "..\nAreas of light rain and drizzle will gradually taper off from NW\nto SE later this afternoon and evening, with mostly to partly cloudy\nskies then lingering through tonight. Where a few breaks in the\nclouds develop, patchy/areas of fog will develop, with most\nlikely conditions within portions of the Hudson/Mohawk Valleys\nand the CT River Valley. As a secondary cold front settles\nsoutheast toward daybreak, a developing light north/northeast\nbreeze may allow any fog to lift prior to daybreak.\n\nOtherwise, strong high pressure will build southward from\neastern Canada through midweek. However, several upper level\ndisturbances tracking south of the region will bring periods of\nhigh/mid level clouds to the region, especially Monday\nafternoon/night into Tuesday morning. There could be a few light\nshowers for areas closer to I-84 during this time time period,\nespecially Monday night/Tuesday AM, however overall chances are\nrather low (20-30%).\n\nPatchy frost will be possible across portions of the SW\nAdirondacks, mainly Tuesday night/Wednesday AM, as a thick veil\nof high clouds may limit colder temps and frost formation for\nMon night/Tue AM.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThere still remains considerable uncertainty regarding the\npotential development and track of a coastal low off the mid\nAtlantic coast, and any impacts (rain/breezy conditions) across\neastern NY/western New England as anticyclonic wave breaking \nleads to the development of a Rex Block pattern, with a closed \nupper low sliding to the south of our region. As it emerges over\nthe Atlantic, coastal cyclogenesis will occur with the \nresulting sfc low tracking somewhere off the east coast. Most \nguidance now keeps our area dry through the end of the week, \nwith any associated rain/breezy conditions from this coastal low\ndelayed until next weekend. Guidance tends to struggle with \nthese blocky flow patterns, and so confidence for this time \nperiod remains quite low. Additional changes to the forecast \nlate-week should be expected.",
